  "step_2": "The individual states: 'Die 100€ Unterschied berührten mich wenig, daher sah ich auf die Wahrscheinlichkeiten. Die warne auch fast gleich, daher hab ich die höhere gewählt.' This translates to: 'The 100€ difference didn't affect me much, so I looked at the probabilities. They were also almost the same, so I chose the higher one.' The individual considers the difference in outcomes (100€) as negligible. Then, they note that the probabilities are almost equal (33% vs. 34%), and based on that, they selected the lottery with the higher payoff (2500€ vs. 2400€). The deliberation process focuses on the small difference in outcomes and the near-equality of probabilities, leading to a choice based on the higher monetary outcome. There is no mention of skewness, distribution of probabilities, or left/right skew. The justification is based on payoff magnitude and probability similarity, not on the shape of the probability distribution or skewness.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason (skewness) is not considered by the individual. The individual does not mention or evaluate the distribution of probabilities across outcomes or whether one lottery has more probability mass on the favorable side. Instead, they focus on the small difference in payoffs and the near-equality of probabilities, leading to a choice based on the higher payoff. The decision reason requires evaluating skewness — specifically, whether a lottery is more left-skewed (favorable outcomes have higher probability). In this case, both lotteries have the favorable outcome with a slightly higher probability in B, so B is slightly more right-skewed, not left-skewed. Thus, the reason does not apply. The individual’s justification is based on payoff magnitude and probability similarity, not on skewness. Therefore, the decision reason was not used. Final assessment: 0.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
